---
import PolicyPage from '../components/PolicyPage.astro';
import { site } from '../lib/site';
---

<PolicyPage path="/privacy" heading="Privacy" updated="2026-11-14">
  <Fragment slot="lede">
    What firenook.dev and the Firenook package collect about you. In short: the site counts visits without cookies, and
    Firenook counts its daily release check by version and platform, and sends nothing else.
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="site">This website</h2>
  <p>
    firenook.dev is a set of files built ahead of time. It sets no cookies, keeps nothing in your browser’s storage except
    the docs’ light or dark choice, and shows no ads. Its fonts and pictures are served from firenook.dev itself, so no
    third party sees your visit through them.
  </p>
  <p>
    To count visits, Cloudflare adds its Web Analytics script to each page. It reports the page, the site that sent you,
    your browser, device and country, and how fast the page loaded, and it uses no cookies or browser storage. It isn’t
    added for visitors in the EU. We see only those totals, never who you are.
  </p>
  <p>
    Cloudflare serves the site. Like any web host it handles your IP address and browser details to deliver each page and
    to protect the site from abuse, under <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">its privacy policy</a>. We do
    not use those logs to follow or profile anyone.
  </p>
  <p>
    Links to GitHub and npm take you to those services, which have their own policies. The docs’ search runs in your
    browser on an index served by this site; what you type is not sent anywhere.
  </p>

  <h2 id="firenook">Firenook</h2>
  <p>
    Firenook runs on your machine. It sends no telemetry, crash reports or anything about your project, and keeps your
    data, logs and diagnostics on your machine, mostly in your project’s <code>.firenook</code> folder.
  </p>
  <p>It reaches the network in two cases:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>A check for a newer release.</strong> At most once a day, the npm package asks which versions of firenook are
      current, in the background, and shows the answer on your next start. From 0.2.5 it asks firenook.dev, and the npm
      registry when firenook.dev does not answer. The request names your Firenook version and platform (such as{' '}
      <code>darwin-arm64</code>), and firenook.dev adds one to that day’s count for them. Nothing else is kept: not your
      address, not an id, nothing about your project, so the count says how many machines checked that day, never which.
      Only we see the daily totals. Before 0.2.5 the check asked the npm registry and nothing was counted. It is off in CI and in the plain output, and <code>FIRENOOK_NO_UPDATE_CHECK=1</code>,{' '}
      <code>NO_UPDATE_NOTIFIER=1</code> or <code>DO_NOT_TRACK=1</code> turns it off anywhere.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>An Extension from the registry,</strong> fetched once the first time it runs. <code>npx firenook ext:vendor</code>
      copies it into your project, so it is never fetched again.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Your own Functions code can reach anything it is written to reach; Firenook is not a network sandbox. A{' '}
    <code>demo-</code> project id never reaches Google’s services.
  </p>

  <h2 id="contact">Questions</h2>
  <p>
    Ask in <a href={site.issues}>the issues on GitHub</a>. For something you would rather not post in public, see{' '}
    <a href="/security">Security</a>.
  </p>
</PolicyPage>
